7.text:
For a just identified homoskedastic model the Nagar first order bias of
2SLS can be written in the familiar form

\begin{equation*}
P\! \lim \,\widehat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\;\approx\;\rho_{uv}\,
\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{v}}\;\frac{1}{\mu^{2}}  ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $\rho_{uv}\sigma_{u}/\sigma_{v}$ scale

\begin{equation*}
{\rm Bias\,rel}\equiv
\frac{{\rm Bias}(\widehat\beta_{2SLS})}{{\rm Bias}(\widehat\beta_{OLS})}
\;\approx\;\frac{k}{\mu^{2}}\approx\frac{1}{F_{0}}\ , 
\end{equation*}
$k=1$ for the one instrument case, and $F_{0}$ is the population
first-stage $F$.  In particular, up to the usual $o_{p}(1)$ terms

\begin{equation*}
 {\rm Bias\,rel}\approx F_{0}^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that in finite samples ${\rm Bias\,rel}\approx F^{-1}$ and the
conventional point estimate of the $10\%$ bias rule is

\begin{equation*}
F_{0}^{-1}\approx0.10\Longleftrightarrow F_{0}\approx10 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the well known $F>10$ rule of thumb.

If $F$ is treated as a random variable the weak instrument limit is

\begin{equation*}
F\;\xrightarrow{d}\;\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda)/1 ,\qquad 
\lambda\approx F_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
with $\mu^{2}\approx\lambda$.  In the normal approximation
$\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda)\approx N\!\left(1+\lambda,\,2\!\left[1+2\lambda
\right]\right)$ the $95\%$ one-sided upper quantile of $F$ under the
null $F_{0}=10$ is

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}\approx \lambda+1+1.645\sqrt{2\,(1+2\lambda)}
     \approx 11+1.645\sqrt{42}\approx 21\!-\!23 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the Stock–Yogo maximal–relative-bias critical value.  In the
Stock–Yogo table this is the $5\%$ maximal relative bias $0.10$ critical
value

\begin{equation*}
c_{0.05}( {\rm RelBias}=0.10)=22.3\approx 22\!-\!23,
\end{equation*}
for one endogenous variable and one instrument.  The $F_{0}$ point
estimate of the $10\%$ Nagar relative bias is, of course, $F_{0}\approx
1/0.10=10$, which is the $F>10$ rule that is usually quoted in practice.
The $F\approx 22$ value is the usual $95\%$--confidence, i.e. maximal
relative–bias, Stock–Yogo critical value.  This is often quoted as
$F\approx 23$.

4.text:
For a just identified homoskedastic model let

* $Y=Z\pi +v$, ${\rm Var}(v)=\sigma _{v}^{2}$,
* $\mu ^{2}= \pi 'Z'Z\pi /\sigma_{v}^{2}$ be the concentration parameter.

On the Nagar mean approximation

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}\big[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\big]\simeq 
\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\frac{L}{\mu^{2}}\;,
\qquad 
{\rm E}\big[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta\big]=\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the Nagar approximation

\begin{equation*}
{\rm RelBias}\;:=\;
\frac{{\rm E}[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta]}
     {{\rm E}[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta]}\simeq  \frac{L}{\mu^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual normalisation ${\rm E}F\simeq 1+\mu^{2}/L$, for
$F\gg 1$

\begin{equation*}
{\rm RelBias}\simeq \frac{1}{F}\;,
\qquad L=1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F\approx \mu^{2}/L$ is the usual $L$th–stage $F$.  Put

\begin{equation*}
{\rm RelBias}_{0}=0.10\;, \qquad 
\mu_{0}^{2}=L/{\rm RelBias}_{0}=10 .
\end{equation*}

For one instrument, up to the usual $n\to\infty$ limit,

\begin{equation*}
F\;\approx\;W^{2},\qquad W\sim N\!\left(\sqrt{\mu^{2}},1\right),
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F$ is (approximately) a non–central $\chi^{2}_{1}(\mu^{2})$.
For a given $\mu^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
{\rm P}\!\left(F>c\mid\mu^{2}\right)
\simeq {\rm P}\!\left(W>\sqrt c\right)
=1-\Phi\!\left(\sqrt c-\sqrt{\mu^{2}}\right) .
\end{equation*}
A $5\%$–right–tail test of $H_{0}:\mu^{2}\le\mu_{0}^{2}$ is therefore
to reject when

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt c-\sqrt{\mu_{0}^{2}}=z_{0.95},
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $N(\sqrt{\mu^{2}},1)$ approximation

\begin{equation*}
c_{\,0.95}\;\approx\;\Bigl(\sqrt{\mu_{0}^{2}}+z_{0.95}\Bigr)^{2},
\qquad 
\mu_{0}^{2}=L/0.10 .
\end{equation*}
With $L=1$, $\sqrt{\mu_{0}^{2}}=\sqrt{10}=3.16$ and $z_{0.95}=1.645$,

\begin{equation*}
c_{0.95}\approx\bigl(3.162+1.645\bigr)^{2}\approx 23.1,
\end{equation*}
so that an observed first stage $F\gtrsim 23$ puts the 95 % one–sided
lower confidence limit on $\mu^{2}$ above the $\mu_{0}^{2}=10$ which
corresponds to a Nagar relative bias of ten per cent.  On the usual
two–sided $1.96$ normal normal approximation

\begin{equation*}
c\approx\bigl(\sqrt{10}+1.96\bigr)^{2}\approx 26.2,
\end{equation*}
and the familiar $F_{\rm obs}-1.645\sqrt{2F_{\rm obs}}\ge 10$ normal
approximation gives

\begin{equation*}
F_{\rm obs}-1.645\sqrt{2F_{\rm obs}}=10\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
F_{\rm obs}\approx20\text{--}21,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F\approx 20$–$25$ in round numbers, the conventional
$F\approx23$ one–sided figure, about $21$ on the $F-1.645\,{\rm s.e.}(F)$
normalisation.  This is the $F>10$ rule of thumb, $F>10$ for an
expected $10\%$ Nagar relative bias, inflated to $F\approx 2$–$2.5\times
10$ once sampling variation in the first stage is allowed for.  The
conventional Stock–Yogo 5 % maximal–size version of the same
calculation is the oft quoted $F\approx16.4$ for a $10\%$ maximal
relative bias.

*Remark.*  With the common two–sided $1.96$ normalisation the 95 %
lower bound $F_{L}=F_{\rm obs}-1.96\sqrt{2F_{\rm obs}}$ gives
$F_{L}=10$ at $F_{\rm obs}\approx23$–$26$, essentially the $F\approx
23$ figure quoted above. ∎

6.text:
With one endogenous regressor and one instrument the Nagar first order
approximation under homoskedasticity can be written in the familiar form

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}\big[\hat\beta_{TSLS}-\beta\big]\approx
-\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\frac{k_{2}}{\mu^{2}},\qquad 
k_{2}=L-2,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}\big[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta\big]\approx
\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\frac{1}{1+\mu^{2}/L}\;, 
\end{equation*}
so that  up to $o_{p}(1)$

\begin{equation*}
\rho_{2SLS}\equiv
\frac{{\rm E}[\hat\beta_{TSLS}-\beta]}{{\rm E}[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta]}
\approx \frac{L}{\mu^{2}+L}\approx \frac{L}{\mu^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the just identified case $L=1$

\begin{equation*}
\big|\rho_{2SLS}\big|\approx \frac{1}{\mu^{2}}\approx\frac{1}{F-1}
\approx\frac1F ,
\end{equation*}
on the usual first–stage scaling  $E[F]=1+\mu^{2}/L$.
Hence in the usual first order Nagar sense

\begin{equation*}
|\rho_{2SLS}|\approx \frac1F ,
\end{equation*}
so that the Nagar $10\%$ relative bias point is

\begin{equation*}
\frac1F\approx 0.10\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad F\approx10,
\end{equation*}
which is the familiar $F>10$ rule, $F-1\approx10$ in the $F-1\approx\mu^{2}$
scaling.

Inverting the usual Stock–Yogo weak–instrument test the $5\%$ size
critical value for a $10\%$ maximal Nagar relative bias with $L=1$
endogenous regressor and $L=1$ instrument is

\begin{equation*}
c_{0.10}\approx 8.96\approx 9,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F\gtrsim 9\!-\!10$ is the conventional $5\%$ test of 
${\rm  max\,rel.\,bias}\le 10\%$.  In the textbook $1/F$ normalisation
this is quoted as $F\approx10$, the $F>10$ rule of thumb.  If the
sampling uncertainty of the first stage $F$ is kept explicitly,
$F_{\rm low}\approx F- z_{0.95}\sqrt{2F}$ is the $95\%$ one–sided
lower bound for the (essentially) $\chi^{2}_{1}(\mu^{2})$ first stage.
Imposing $F_{\rm low}-1\approx10$ gives $F-1.645\sqrt{2F}\approx10$,
i.e. $F\approx 20\!-\!23$, about $F\approx21$ with the $1.645$
one-sided $95\%$ normal approximation, the $F\approx23$ often quoted
for the $1.96$ two-sided $95\%$ bound.  This is the $F\approx10$ rule
in the usual point–Nagar normalisation.

2.text:
In a just identified homoskedastic model the Nagar first order bias can be
written in the familiar form

\begin{equation*}
{\rm Bias}\;\widehat\beta_{2SLS}\;\approx\;
\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\frac{L}{\mu},
\qquad 
\mu=\frac{\pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\pi}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}

and ${\rm Bias}\;\widehat\beta_{OLS}\approx\sigma_{uv}/\sigma_{v}^{2}$ up to
the usual $O(\mu^{-1})$ terms.  Hence on the scale which is usually quoted

\begin{equation*}
R\equiv
\frac{{\rm Bias}\;\widehat\beta_{2SLS}}
     {{\rm Bias}\;\widehat\beta_{OLS}}
 \approx \frac{L}{\mu}\approx\frac{1}{F_{1S}} ,
\end{equation*}

$L=1$ in the just identified case and $F_{1S}$ is the usual first-stage
F.  In particular $R\approx F_{1S}^{-1}$ and  $E[R]\approx F_{1S}^{-1}$.
Put $\widehat R_{0}=F_{1S}^{-1}$.  To first order

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{{\rm Var}\big(\widehat R_{0}\big)}
      \approx\frac{{\rm Var}\big(F_{1S}\big)^{1/2}}{F_{1S}^{2}}
      \approx\frac{\sqrt{2F_{1S}}}{F_{1S}^{2}}
      =\frac{\sqrt2}{F_{1S}^{3/2}},
\end{equation*}

so that with the normal approximation

\begin{equation*}
{\rm UB}_{0.95}\big(R\big)\approx
\frac1{F}
+ z_{0.95}\frac{\sqrt2}{F^{3/2}}  \;<\;0.10 .
\end{equation*}

Writing $t=\sqrt{F}$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac1{t^{2}}+\frac{z_{0.95}\sqrt2}{t^{3}}=0.10 ,
\qquad 
0.1t^{3}-t-z_{0.95}\sqrt2=0,
\end{equation*}

i.e. with the one–sided $z_{0.95}=1.645$

\begin{equation*}
t^{3}-10t-23.26=0,
\qquad t\approx3.98,
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}\approx t^{2}\approx 1.5\!-\!1.7\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}

about $F\approx 15$–$17$.  With the conventional two–sided $z_{0.975}=1.96$
the 95 % upper confidence bound is

\[
\frac1F+\frac{1.96\sqrt2}{F^{3/2}}=0.10\;\Longleftrightarrow\;F\approx
16\!-\!17,
\]

the well known Stock–Yogo 10 % maximal–relative–bias calibration,
$F_{SY}(10\%,L=1)\approx 16.4$, which is the $F\gtrsim10$ rule of thumb
with the usual finite–sample normal correction.  Ignoring the normal
correction the point–estimate rule is $R\approx1/F<0.10\iff F>10$.

Remark.  If the $95\%$ lower confidence bound is required for the
concentration parameter itself, $\mu_{L}=F-1-1.645\sqrt{2F}$, the
requirement $\mu_{L}>L/0.10=10$ gives the more conservative non–central
$F$ calibration $F\approx 20\!-\!24$, $F\approx23$ with the
$(\sqrt\mu+z_{0.95})^{2}$ approximation, i.e. the usual Stock–Yogo 5 %
size critical value $F_{c}\approx16.4$ for the maximal size of the
2SLS Wald test.  This is the $F>10$ rule quoted in the literature.

